"Characterizing, Evaluating, and Optimizing Complex Reasoning (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08498v1)\nciteturn31796view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31795view0\",\"lineno\":527}); Total lines: 894\nL396:   * Yan et al. (2025) J. Yan, Y. Li, Z. Hu, Z. Wang, G. Cui, X. Qu, Y. Cheng, and Y. Zhang Learning to reason under off-policy guidance. arXiv preprint arXiv:2504.14945. Cited by: cite194†§G.3 .\nL397:   * Yang et al. (2025) A. Yang, A. Li, B. Yang, B. Zhang, B. Hui, B. Zheng, B. Yu, C. Gao, C. Huang, C. Lv, et al. Qwen3 technical report. arXiv preprint arXiv:2505.09388. Cited by: cite164†Appendix F , cite165†§5.1 , cite166†§5.3 .\nL398:   * Yang et al. (2024) A. Yang, B. Zhang, B. Hui, B. Gao, B. Yu, C. Li, D. Liu, J. Tu, J. Zhou, J. Lin, et al. Qwen2. 5-math technical report: toward mathematical expert model via self-improvement. arXiv preprint arXiv:2409.12122. Cited by: cite173†§5.4 .\nL399:   * Zeng et al. (2025) W. Zeng, Y. Huang, Q. Liu, W. Liu, K. He, Z. Ma, and J. He Simplerl-zoo: investigating and taming zero reinforcement learning for open base models in the wild. arXiv preprint arXiv:2503.18892. Cited by: cite195†§1 , cite175†§5.4 .\nL400:   * Zhan et al. (2025) R. Zhan, Y. Li, Z. Wang, X. Qu, D. Liu, J. Shao, D. F. Wong, and Y. Cheng ExGRPO: learning to reason from experience. arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.02245. Cited by: cite174†§1 .\nL401:   * Zhang et al. (2025a) Y. Zhang, I. Kuzborskij, J. D. Lee, C. Leng, and F. Liu DAG-math: graph-guided mathematical reasoning in llms. arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.19842. Cited by: cite180†§2 .\nL402:   * Zhang et al. (2025b) Z. Zhang, C. Zheng, Y. Wu, B. Zhang, R. Lin, B. Yu, D. Liu, J. Zhou, and J. Lin The lessons of developing process reward models in mathematical reasoning. arXiv preprint arXiv:2501.07301. Cited by: cite188†§C.1 , cite192†§5.2 , cite166†§5.3 , cite204†§5.3 .\nL403:   * Zhao et al. (2025) S. Zhao, J. Yuan, J. Wu, Z. Wang, G. Yang, and U. Naseem Can pruning improve reasoning? revisiting long-cot compression with capability in mind for better reasoning. arXiv preprint arXiv:2505.14582. Cited by: cite182†§3.2 .\nL404:   * Zou et al. (2025) J. Zou, L. Yang, J. Gu, J. Qiu, K. Shen, J. He, and M. Wang ReasonFlux-prm: trajectory-aware prms for long chain-of-thought reasoning in llms. arXiv preprint arXiv:2506.18896. Cited by: cite205†§C.1 , cite194†§G.3 , cite174†§1 , cite167†§1 , cite177†§2 , cite181†§2 , cite192†§5.2 , cite166†§5.3 , cite204†§5.3 , cite206†§6.2 .\nL405: ## Appendix Contents\nL406: \nL407: This appendix provides supplementary theoretical analyses, methodological details, experimental settings, and qualitative case studies that support and extend the main paper.\nL408: \nL409:   * •\nL410: \nL411: cite48†A : Optimal Policy Invariance of TRM-Guided Reward Shaping\nL412: \nL413:   * •\nL414: \nL415: cite49†B : Lower Bound on Policy Improvement\nL416: \nL417:     * –\nL418: \nL419: cite50†B.1 : Notations\nL420: \nL421:     * –\nL422: \nL423: cite51†B.2 : Problem Formulation and Preliminary Lemmas\nL424: \nL425:     * –\nL426: \nL427: cite52†B.3 : Proof of Theorem cite207†2 L428: \nL429:   * •\nL430: \nL431: cite53†C : Discussion\nL432: \nL433:     * –\nL434: cite54†C.1 : Discussion on ReasonFlux-PRM and our TRM\nL435: \nL436:     * –\nL437: \nL438: cite58†C.2 : Discussion on Self-Refine\nL439: \nL440:     * –\nL441: \nL442: cite59†C.3 : Discussion on Robust Evaluation\nL443: \nL444:   * •\nL445: \nL446: cite60†D : Details of Step Partitioning\nL447: \nL448:   * •\nL449: \nL450: cite61†E : Details of DAG Construction\nL451: \nL452:   * •\nL453: \nL454: cite65†F : Details of Preference Dataset Construction\nL455: \nL456:   * •\nL457: \nL458: cite71†G : Experimental Details\nL459: \nL460:     * –\nL461: \nL462: cite72†G.1 : Thinking Reward Model Training\nL463: \nL464:     * –\nL465: \nL466: cite73†G.2 : Test-Time Scaling\nL467: \nL468:     * –\nL469: \nL470: cite74†G.3 : RL Optimization\nL471: \nL472:   * •\nL473: \nL474: cite75†H : Ablation Study\nL475: \nL476:   * •\nL477: cite76†I : Brief Introduction of Bradley–Terry Loss\nL478: \nL479:   * •\nL480: \nL481: cite77†J : Prompt Design\nL482: \nL483:     * –\nL484: \nL485: cite78†J.1 : DAG Construction Prompt\nL486: \nL487:     * –\nL488: \nL489: cite79†J.2 : DAG Linearization for Macro-level Evaluation\nL490: \nL491:     * –\nL492: \nL493: cite80†J.3 : Dominant Path Extraction for Micro-level Evaluation\nL494: \nL495:     * –\nL496: \nL497: cite81†J.4 : Evaluation Prompt\nL498: \nL499:   * •\nL500: \nL501: cite82†K : Case Study\nL502: \nL503:     * –\nL504: \nL505: cite83†K.1 : DAG Structure\nL506: \nL507:     * –\nL508: \nL509: cite86†K.2 : Reasoning Trace Comparison (Case 1)\nL510: \nL511:     * –\nL512: \nL513: cite87†K.3 : Reasoning Trace Comparison (Case 2)\nL514: \nL515:     * –\nL516: cite88†K.4 : Reasoning Trace Comparison (Case 3)\nL517: \nL518:     * –\nL519: \nL520: cite89†K.5 : Reasoning Trace Comparison (Case 4)\nL521: ## Appendix A Optimal Policy Invariance of TRM-Guided Reward Shaping\nL522: \nL523: Ensuring the optimal policy invariance requires a potential-based reward shaping scheme (cite208†Ng et al., 1999 ; cite209†Wang et al., 2023 ; cite210†Müller and Kudenko, 2025 ). The original reward shaping scheme is:\nL524: \nL525:  | $$r=r_{v}\\cdot\\left(1-\\alpha+\\alpha\\cdot\\text{Sigmoid}(r_{t})\\right).$$  |  | (3)\nL526: \nL527: Since $r_{v}\\in\\{0,1\\}$, the shaping scheme in Eq. cite211†3 can be converted to another form:\nL528:  | $$r=(1-\\alpha)\\cdot r_{v}+\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbb{I}(r_{v}=1)\\cdot\\text{Sigmoid}(r_{t}).$$  |  | (4)\nL529: \nL530: Let $\\Phi(\\cdot):S\\to\\mathbb{R}$ denote a real-valued potential function over the state space $s\\in S$, i.e., $\\Phi(s)$ is the potential of state $s$. We formulate the shaping reward $R_{s}$ as the difference between the potentials of adjacent states as\nL531: \nL532:  | $$R_{s}(s_{t},a_{t},s_{t+1})=\\gamma\\Phi(s_{t+1})-\\Phi(s_{t}).$$  |  | (5)\nL533: In the LLM reasoning setting, the states and action within a given query-response pair are defined as $s_{t}:=[q,o_{i,\\leq t}]$, $a_{t}:=o_{i,t+1}$, and $s_{t+1}:=[q,o_{i,\\leq t+1}]$. We can take the potential function to be the thinking bonus $\\text{TRM}(\\cdot)$ for any intermediate state $s_{t}$ of the complete reasoning chain:\nL534: \nL535:  | $$\\Phi(s_{t}):=\\mathbb{I}(r_{v}=1)\\cdot\\text{TRM}(s_{t}).$$  |  | (6)\nL536: \nL537: The shaping reward becomes:\nL538:  | $$R_{s}\\left([q,o_{i,\\leq t}],o_{i,t+1},[q,o_{i,\\leq t+1}]\\right)=\\mathbb{I}(r_{v}=1)\\cdot\\left(\\gamma\\text{TRM}([q,o_{i,\\leq t+1}])-\\text{TRM}([q,o_{i,\\leq t}])\\right).$$  |  | (7)\nL539: \nL540: In GRPO settings, policy gradients are derived at the sequence level. Then, the shaping reward for a complete query-response pair is calculated as\nL541:  | $\\displaystyle R_{s}\\Bigl([q,o_{i}]\\Bigr)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\\gamma^{t}R_{s}\\Bigl([q,o_{i,\\leq t}],o_{i,t+1},[q,o_{i,\\leq t+1}]\\Bigr)$  |  | (8)\nL542:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\\gamma^{t}\\mathbb{I}(r_{v}=1)[\\gamma\\text{TRM}([q,o_{i,\\leq t+1}])-\\text{TRM}([q,o_{i,\\leq t}])]$  |\nL543:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\gamma^{T}\\cdot\\mathbb{I}(r_{v}=1)\\cdot\\text{TRM}([q,o_{i,\\leq T}])-\\mathbb{I}(r_{v}=1)\\cdot\\text{TRM}(q)$  |\nL544:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\gamma^{T}\\cdot\\mathbb{I}(r_{v}=1)\\cdot\\text{TRM}([q,o_{i}]),$  |\nL545: where the think raward bonus is zero for the query $q$, i.e., $\\text{TRM}(q)=0$ That is, the shaping reward for a complete query-response pair is derived as the thinking reward bonus of the final response. This formulation avoids the need to calculate the thinking reward bonus of any intermediate sentences.\nL546: \nL547: Finally, we use the new reward function as\nL548:  | $\\displaystyle r$  | $\\displaystyle=(1-\\alpha)\\cdot r_{v}+\\alpha\\cdot R_{s}([q,o_{i}])$  |  | (9)\nL549:  |  | $\\displaystyle=(1-\\alpha)\\cdot r_{v}+\\alpha\\cdot\\gamma^{T}\\cdot\\mathbb{I}(r_{v}=1)\\cdot\\text{TRM}([q,o_{i}])$  |\nL550:  |  | $\\displaystyle=(1-\\alpha)\\cdot r_{v}+\\alpha\\cdot\\gamma^{T}\\cdot\\mathbb{I}(r_{v}=1)\\cdot\\text{Sigmoid}(r_{t}).$  |\nL551: \nL552: That is the reason why we change the original reward in Eq. cite211†3 to a new form as in Eq. cite212†4 .\nL553: ###### Theorem 1 (Optimal Policy Invariance).\nL554: Let $M\\!=\\!(S,A,T,R,\\gamma)$ denote the MDP for the LLM reasoning task. $\\text{TRM}(\\cdot)\\!:S\\mapsto\\mathbb{R}$ is a real-valued function that computes the thinking reward bonus $\\text{TRM}(s)$ of the state $s$ within a group of responses.\nL555: We formulate $R_{s}(\\cdot)\\!:S\\!\\times\\!A\\!\\times\\!S\\mapsto\\mathbb{R}$ as a shaping reward function that is the difference between thinking reward bonuses of two adjacent states, such that for all $s\\in S,a\\in A,s^{\\prime}\\in S$, $R_{s}(s,a,s^{\\prime})=\\gamma\\text{TRM}(s^{\\prime})-\\text{TRM}(s)$. Then, with any constant balancing ratio $\\alpha$, every optimal policy in the transformed MDP $M^{\\prime}\\!=\\!(S,A,T,R+\\alpha R_{s},\\gamma)$ will also be an optimal policy in $M$, and vice versa.\nL556: ###### Proof.\nL557: \nL558: For the original MDP $M$, we know that its optimal Q-function $Q_{M}^{*}$ satisfies the Bellman optimality equation (cite213†Sutton and Barto, 2018 ):\nL559: \nL560:  | $$Q_{M}^{*}(s,a)=\\mathbb{E}_{s^{\\prime}}\\left[R(s,a,s^{\\prime})+\\gamma\\max_{a^{\\prime}\\in A}Q_{M}^{*}(s^{\\prime},a^{\\prime})\\right].$$  |  | (10)\nL561: \nL562: With some simple algebraic manipulation, we can get:\nL563:  | $$Q_{M}^{*}(s,a)-\\alpha\\text{TRM}(s)=\\mathbb{E}_{s^{\\prime}}\\left[R(s,a,s^{\\prime})+\\alpha\\Bigl(\\gamma\\text{TRM}(s^{\\prime})-\\text{TRM}(s)\\Bigr)+\\gamma\\max_{a^{\\prime}\\in A}\\Bigl(Q_{M}^{*}(s^{\\prime},a^{\\prime})-\\alpha\\text{TRM}(s^{\\prime})\\Bigr)\\right].$$  |  | (11)\nL564: \nL565: If we now define $\\hat{Q}_{M^{\\prime}}(s,a)\\triangleq Q_{M}^{*}(s,a)-\\alpha\\text{TRM}(s)$ and substitute that and $R_{s}(s,a,s^{\\prime})=\\gamma\\text{TRM}(s^{\\prime})-\\text{TRM}(s)$ into the previous equation, we can get:\nL566:  | $\\displaystyle\\hat{Q}_{M^{\\prime}}(s,a)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{s^{\\prime}}\\left[R(s,a,s^{\\prime})+\\alpha R_{s}(s,a,s^{\\prime})+\\gamma\\max_{a^{\\prime}\\in A}\\hat{Q}_{M^{\\prime}}(s^{\\prime},a^{\\prime})\\right]$  |  | (12)\nL567:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\mathbb{E}_{s^{\\prime}}\\left[R^{\\prime}(s,a,s^{\\prime})+\\gamma\\max_{a^{\\prime}\\in A}\\hat{Q}_{M^{\\prime}}(s^{\\prime},a^{\\prime})\\right],$  |\nL568: which is exactly the Bellman optimality equation for the transformed MDP $M^{\\prime}$, where $R^{\\prime}=R+\\alpha R_{s}$ is the reward function for $M^{\\prime}$. Thus, $Q_{M^{\\prime}}^{*}(s,a)=\\hat{Q}_{M^{\\prime}}(s,a)=Q_{M}^{*}(s,a)-\\alpha\\text{TRM}(s)$, and the optimal policy for $M^{\\prime}$ therefore satisfies:\nL569:  | $\\displaystyle\\pi_{M^{\\prime}}^{*}(s)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\arg\\max_{a\\in A}Q_{M^{\\prime}}^{*}(s,a)$  |  | (13)\nL570:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\arg\\max_{a\\in A}\\Bigl[Q_{M}^{*}(s,a)-\\alpha\\text{TRM}(s)\\Bigr]$  |\nL571:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\arg\\max_{a\\in A}Q_{M}^{*}(s,a),$  |\nL572: and is therefore also optimal in $M$. To show every optimal policy in $M$ is also optimal in $M^{\\prime}$, simply apply the same proof with the roles of $M$ and $M^{\\prime}$ interchanged (and using $-R_{s}$ as the shaping reward). This completes the proof. ∎\nL573: ## Appendix B Lower Bound on Policy Improvement\nL574: ###### Theorem 2 (Lower Bound on Policy Improvement).\nL575: \nL576: For the base policy iterate $\\pi_{t}$, after one update step with learning rate $\\beta\\ll 1$, there exists a constant $C>0$, such that over state distribution $\\rho$ the following holds:\nL577:  | $$\\mathbb{E}_{s\\sim\\rho}[V^{\\pi_{t+1}}(s)-V^{\\pi_{t}}(s)]\\geq C\\beta\\cdot\\underbrace{\\mathbb{E}_{s\\sim\\rho}\\mathbb{V}_{a\\sim\\pi_{t}}[\\alpha A^{\\text{TRM}}(s,a)]}_{\\text{distinguishability from TRM}}+C\\beta\\cdot\\underbrace{\\mathbb{E}_{s\\sim\\rho}\\mathbb{E}_{a\\sim\\pi_{t}}[\\alpha A^{\\text{TRM}}(s,a)A^{\\pi_{t}}(s,a)]}_{\\text{alignment between }\\pi_{t}\\text{ and TRM}}$$  |  | (14)\nL578: The first term in the lower bound formalizes that policy improvement is directly proportional to the variance of the reward signal. If the reward signal cannot distinguish between different actions or trajectories (low variance), the policy update becomes stagnant or inefficient. In standard RL for reasoning (like RLVR), all correct traces receive a reward of $1$, and all incorrect ones receive $0$.\nL579: This creates a signal plateau for correct answers, that is, the model cannot distinguish a high-quality, efficient proof from a redundant, hallucinatory but ultimately correct one.\nL580: In our method, we include a think reward $r_{t}$ that can introduce variance among successful trajectories. The theorem suggests that this increased distinguishability allows the model to more effectively push the model toward the most efficient and effective reasoning patterns under the proposed ME^{2} principle, rather than any path that hits the right answer.\nL581: The second term in the lower bound indicates that our TRM should not be too misaligned with the base policy $\\pi$; otherwise, $\\mathbb{E}[\\langle\\alpha A^{\\text{TRM}},A^{\\pi_{t}}\\rangle]$ would be too negative. Since TRM rewards are only added to correct solutions, our method naturally follows the theoretical requirement of not being misaligned with the primary goal of accuracy.\nL582: By distinguishing valid pathways without incentivizing incorrect solutions, the TRM $r_{t}$ functions as an auxiliary advantage $A^{\\text{TRM}}$ that is congruent with the primary advantage $A^{\\pi_{t}}$. This alignment drives the policy toward more advantageous paths among those leading to the truth, guided by the ME^{2} principles.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCharacterizing, Evaluating, and Optimizing Complex Reasoning (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08498v1)\nciteturn31796view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31795view0\",\"lineno\":777}); Total lines: 894\nL762: #### Rationale and Complexity Analysis.\nL763: Compared to using all prior nodes, the simplified attachment pool reduces the candidate set size from $O(n)$ to $O(d+b)$, where $n$ is the total number of steps processed so far, $d$ is the depth of the current main branch, and $b$ is the number of retained branch endpoints. In practice, both $d$ and $b$ are smaller than $n$, leading to notable reductions in prompt length and inference cost.\nL764: While this design necessarily omits some older and inactive nodes, our empirical analysis on reasoning traces from multiple model families suggests that most parent-child relations arise from either (i) local continuation along the current main branch, or (ii) reconnection to the most recent nodes of alternative branches. Under these conditions, the simplified attachment pool captures the majority of semantically meaningful dependencies observed in practice.\nL765: As a result, although it does not preserve the full historical context of the reasoning trace, this design provides a reasonable approximation to the complete DAG context in our experimental settings. We find that itis sufficient in practice for recovering accurate reasoning structures and serves as a key component enabling scalable and reliable modeling of reasoning DAGs.\nL766: ## Appendix F Details of Preference Dataset Construction\nL767: \nL768: In this section, we describe the detailed procedure for constructing the preference dataset used in our experiments. The judgment steps involved in verification, structural annotation, and pairwise evaluation are performed using DeepSeek-V3.2 (cite136†Liu et al., 2025 ) as the judge model.\nL769: #### Response Generation and Verification.\nL770: We sample 64K prompts from the WebInstruct-verified dataset (cite111†Ma et al., 2025 ) and generate candidate reasoning traces using multiple open-source reasoning models from different model families.\nL771: Specifically, for the Qwen3 family (cite134†Yang et al., 2025 ), we use Qwen3-1.7B, Qwen3-8B, and Qwen3-32B; for the DeepSeek-Distill family (cite92†Guo et al., 2025 ), we use DeepSeek-Distill-Llama-8B and DeepSeek-Distill-Llama-70B; and for the GPT-OSS family (cite135†Agarwal et al., 2025 ), we use GPT-OSS-20B and GPT-OSS-120B. For all models, we set the maximum generation length to 8192 tokens, with temperature 0.7 and top-$p$ 0.95. For each prompt–model pair, we sample $n=4$ reasoning traces.\nL772: To ensure answer correctness, we verify all generated responses using the same verifier prompt as in cite111†Ma et al. (2025) , and retain only verified-correct reasoning traces for subsequent pairwise evaluation. This filtering step is introduced to decouple reasoning quality from final answer correctness, allowing the preference judgments to focus on the properties emphasized by ME^{2} principle rather than being dominated by outcome-level errors.\nL773: #### Motivation for Correctness Filtering.\nL774: In preliminary analysis, we observe that when incorrect answers are included, the judge model may face ambiguous trade-offs, for example between a reasoning trace that largely follows ME^{2} principle but contains a minor error leading to an incorrect final answer, and another trace that violates ME^{2} principle by relying on illogical shortcuts or spurious mathematical justifications yet happens to arrive at the correct answer.\nL775: In such cases, correctness can overshadow structural and procedural reasoning quality, introducing noise and inconsistency into pairwise judgments. Restricting evaluation to verified-correct traces mitigates this effect and yields a cleaner supervision signal for comparing reasoning quality under a shared correctness constraint.\nL776: #### Step Partitioning.\nL777: \nL778: As shown in App. cite60†D , we analyze the empirical distribution of step-prefix patterns across reasoning traces from different model families and identify a small set of frequent and semantically indicative keywords that tend to mark the start of a new reasoning step. Using these keywords, we segment each reasoning trace into a sequence of atomic steps, applying family-specific keyword sets to account for stylistic differences across model outputs.\nL779: #### DAG Construction.\nL780: \nL781: Based on the resulting step sequences, we construct reasoning DAGs according to the procedure outlined in Sec. cite20†4.2 . Parent–child relations between steps are assigned incrementally using LLM-based selection over the attachment pool. The constructed DAGs provide the structured representations used in subsequent macro- and micro-level pairwise evaluation.\nL782: #### Pairwise Evaluation.\nL783: \nL784: We evaluate the processed reasoning traces using the procedure described in Sec. cite21†4.3 , operating on their corresponding DAG representations. All pairwise comparisons follow the hierarchical evaluation protocol and are conducted multiple times under both original and reversed presentation orders (three evaluations per order) to ensure robustness.\nL785: \nL786: ## Appendix G Experimental Details\nL787: ### G.1 Thinking Reward Model Training\nL788: We train the Thinking Reward Model (TRM) using the trl RewardTrainer^{2}^{2} 2 cite236†https://huggingface.co/docs/trl/main/en/reward_trainer†huggingface.co . Training is performed for one epoch with 12 gradient accumulation steps, resulting in a total batch size of 192. We use a learning rate of $1\\times 10^{-6}$ with a cosine scheduler and a warmup ratio of 0.03.\nL789: To stabilize training, we set the reward centering coefficient to 0.001, which constrains the mean reward during optimization and improves numerical stability. The total training time is approximately 20 hours, and the resulting training dynamics are shown in Fig. cite237†9 .\nL790: cite238†Image: Refer to caption Figure 9: Training loss and validation accuracy during TRM training. The training loss converges to around 0.30, and the validation accuracy plateaus at 88.6%, suggesting stable optimization and strong generalization performance.\nL791: ### G.2 Test-Time Scaling\nL792: We use Qwen3-8B and GPT-OSS-20B as generator models. For each prompt $x$, the generator samples $N$ candidate outputs at temperature $T=0.6$ and $\\text{top-p}=0.95$, where $N\\in\\{1,2,4,8,16\\}$. Each candidate consists of a reasoning trace and its final response. TRM scores candidates using only the reasoning trace, producing a scalar thinking reward. In contrast, PRMs (Qwen2.5-Math-7B-PRM and ReasonFlux-PRM-7B) score each candidate using both the reasoning trace and the final response.\nL793: For each method, we select the candidate with the highest reward and report accuracy of the selected outputs.\nL794: ### G.3 RL Optimization\nL795: We implement GRPO training using verl^{3}^{3} 3 cite239†https://github.com/volcengine/verl†github.com and train three policy models. We use a learning rate of $5\\times 10^{-7}$, PPO minibatch size 128, training batch size 512, and PPO clip ratio 0.3. For each prompt, we sample 8 responses at temperature 1.0 and train for 300 steps. We use General-Verifier (cite111†Ma et al., 2025 ) with math verification as the verifier.\nL796: For the reward signal of ReasonFlux-PRM-7B (cite97†Zou et al., 2025 ), we follow the paper’s aggregation, which combines the step-level reward and trajectory-level reward. Following cite240†Yan et al. (2025) , we use the following system prompt for Qwen2.5-Math-1.5B and Qwen2.5-Math-7B:\nL797: For the comparatively weaker Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, we use a simplified system prompt:\nL798: ## Appendix H Ablation Study\nL799: \nL800: Table 4: Ablation study of the weighting coefficient $\\alpha$ on Qwen2.5-Math-7B.\nL801: $\\boldsymbol{\\alpha}$  | STEM Performance (%)  | Math Performance (%)\nL802: GPQA  | SuperGPQA  | MMLU-Pro  | BBEH  |    FS  | Avg.  | AMC  | AIME24  | AIME25  | MATH500  | Olympiad  | Avg.\nL803: 0.8  | 37.9  | 25.2  | 48.5  | 9.3  | 19.6  | 28.1  | 61.9  | 13.2  | 15.1  | 82.0  | 46.8  | 43.8\nL804: 0.5  | 39.9  | 26.0  | 49.9  | 10.4  | 23.9  | 30.0  | 65.6  | 18.3  | 14.9  | 81.4  | 48.3  | 45.7\nL805: 0.2 (ours)  | 42.4  | 26.7  | 50.5  | 9.5  | 26.5  | 31.1  | 63.4  | 19.1  | 17.1  | 83.6  | 47.4  | 46.1\nL806: Tab. cite241†4 reports an ablation on the weighting coefficient $\\alpha$ in Eq. cite147†1 conducted on Qwen2.5-Math-7B. Among the three tested values $\\{0.2,0.5,0.8\\}$, $\\alpha=0.2$ yields the best average performance on both STEM and math, while larger $\\alpha$ values lead to lower averages.\nL807: This suggests that a relatively small $\\alpha$ can provide a reasonable balance in practice, keeping the verifier signal as the primary driver for outcome correctness while allowing TRM to shape and rank alternative verified-correct reasoning traces.\nL808: ## Appendix I Brief Introduction of Bradley–Terry Loss\nL809: \nL810: The Bradley–Terry (BT) model is a standard probabilistic model for pairwise comparisons (cite138†Bradley and Terry, 1952 ). Given two candidates $a$ and $b$ (e.g., two reasoning traces under the same prompt $x$), a reward model $r_{\\theta}(x,\\cdot)$ assigns each candidate a scalar score, and the BT model defines the preference probability as\nL811:  | $$P_{\\theta}(a\\succ b\\mid x)=\\frac{\\exp\\!\\big(r_{\\theta}(x,a)\\big)}{\\exp\\!\\big(r_{\\theta}(x,a)\\big)+\\exp\\!\\big(r_{\\theta}(x,b)\\big)}=\\sigma\\!\\big(r_{\\theta}(x,a)-r_{\\theta}(x,b)\\big),$$  |  | (47)\nL812: where $\\sigma(\\cdot)$ is the sigmoid function. This formulation has become the default objective for reward modeling with pairwise preference data in RLHF (cite107†Stiennon et al., 2020 ; cite242†Ouyang et al., 2022 ). Given a preference dataset $\\mathcal{D}=\\{(x,a_{w},a_{l})\\}$ with winner $a_{w}$ and loser $a_{l}$, the BT loss is the negative log-likelihood:\nL813:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{BT}}(\\theta)=-\\mathbb{E}_{(x,a_{w},a_{l})\\sim\\mathcal{D}}\\left[\\log\\sigma\\!\\big(r_{\\theta}(x,a_{w})-r_{\\theta}(x,a_{l})\\big)\\right],$$  |  | (48)\nL814: \nL815: which encourages the model to assign higher scores to preferred candidates while avoiding unreliable prompt-based absolute scoring.\nL816: ## Appendix J Prompt Design\nL817: \nL818: ### J.1 DAG Construction Prompt\nL819: \nL820: Fig. cite243†16 and Fig. cite244†17 show the prompt used for DAG construction. We construct the DAG in a step-by-step manner: for each newly processed step $v_{i}$, we provide the judge model with an attachment pool of previously seen steps and ask it to decide the connection type and the parent step IDs.\nL821: ### J.2 DAG Linearization for Macro-level Evaluation\nL822: For macro-level evaluation, we convert each super-node DAG into a linearized textual abstraction. Each super-node corresponds to a merged span of reasoning steps that is semantically coherent, and is summarized into a single sentence. The super-nodes are arranged according to their original emission order, which aligns with the stepwise generation process and yields a consistent topological ordering.\nL823: For each super-node, we attach a concise structural header indicating its role in the DAG, including whether it functions as a root, branching, merging, or terminal node, together with its depth and parent–child relations.\nL824: This abstraction exposes the global organization of the reasoning trace in a compact manner. Structural patterns such as the introduction of alternative solution paths, their expansion through branching, and their eventual consolidation or termination become directly observable at the text level. As illustrated in Fig. cite245†10 , branching nodes mark points where the reasoning diverges, while merge and leaf nodes reflect how intermediate conclusions are reconciled or finalized.\nL825: The resulting abstraction removes low-level step content while preserving the structural signals required for macro-level comparison, and is used as input to the macro-level pairwise evaluation prompts.\nL826: Figure 10: A simple example of a macro-level abstraction obtained by linearizing a DAG-structured reasoning trace, where each block is prefixed with a structural tag (e.g., <Branch id=6 depth=3 parents=[4] children=[7,8]> indicates a branching super-node at depth 3 that expands the reasoning from node 4 into two subsequent paths).\nL827: ### J.3 Dominant Path Extraction for Micro-level Evaluation\n"
